# Claude Debussy -- Arabesque No. 1 in E major, L. 66 (1888). Public domain.
#
# A simplified reduction of the opening, not a bar-exact transcription: the
# E-major arpeggio wave, then the pentatonic theme in even eighths over
# left-hand triplets.
#
# That 3:2 cross-rhythm is why this is the most demanding score here. The two
# hands almost never land together, so the reconstruction is asked to hold two
# unrelated fits in the air at once, and the arpeggios mean each one only gets
# a third of a beat to establish itself before the next takes over.

tempo 108
meter 4

track arpeggio
  # Bars 1-2: the wave. Triplet eighths, twelve to the bar.
  E3:1/3 G#3 B3 E4 G#4 B4 E5 G#5 B5 G#5 E5 B4
  E3 G#3 B3 E4 G#4 B4 E5 G#5 B5 G#5 E5 B4
  # Bars 3-6: it settles into accompaniment and stays in triplets under the tune.
  E3 B3 E4 G#4 E4 B3 E3 B3 E4 G#4 E4 B3
  C#3 G#3 C#4 E4 C#4 G#3 C#3 G#3 C#4 E4 C#4 G#3
  A2 E3 A3 C#4 A3 E3 A2 E3 A3 C#4 A3 E3
  B2 F#3 B3 D#4 B3 F#3 B2 F#3 B3 D#4 B3 F#3

track theme gain 0.85
